.TH VAULT-GET 1 "September 2026" "vault" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vault-get \- decrypt a stored file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vault get
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR OUTPUT ]
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-vault
.IR DIR ]
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Decrypts \fINAME\fR. Nothing appears at the output path until every object has
authenticated. The stored mode and modification time are restored. It refuses
to overwrite an existing file. Takes a shared lock.
.PP
With \fB\-r\fR, \fINAME\fR is a folder, and every file under it is
decrypted into the output directory, keeping the folder structure. Each file
is checked as above; a clash stops the run, leaving the files already
written.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " OUTPUT"
Output path, or with \fB\-r\fR output directory. Default: the base name of
\fINAME\fR in the current directory.
.TP
.B \-r
Decrypt a folder and everything under it.
.PP
See also \fB\-\-key\fR and \fB\-\-vault\fR in
.BR vault (1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VAULT_PASSPHRASE
Passphrase, instead of prompting on the terminal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vault (1),
.BR vault\-list (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Andy Dixon <vault@dixon.cx>, https://www.dixon.cx
